\f4\fs26 RELIGION is SLAVERY\
The Sages arrived in The Quarter as conquering heroes returning from battle. Several hundred metres of the main thoroughfare was now gleaming and Elvi were lined either side ten deep, delighted and cheering, each desperate to touch the cloak of one of the prophets. A small lectern had been set up, just outside Parliament House (the old brothel), in case one of the Sages wished to delight the crowd with a few words of praise.\
\'93Defenders of the Dawkins, the cosmos is all that is, ever was or ever will be.\'94 Kleo bellowed out to the mass of mesmerized Elvi who cheered madly upon hearing the great sage\'92s wise words. \'93We are overjoyed by your welcome and look forward to spreading the word of science and reason with you all.\'94 Kleo look out upon the sea of faces, all glowing with love for The Dawkins. \'93Science and Reason pave the way towards the truth, but remember, there is no ultimate truth. Praise be to his name.\'94\
\'93We are well versed in the the Chronicles of the Dawkins, wise one.\'94 A lone Elvis was heard above the excitement. \'93We are told you were once a disciple of the false prophet, Jameson Hardy.\'94 \
\'93It is true, but I am proud to say I was in the employ of such a great man. At one time I did, indeed, think him to be the one-after-the-one, but he never made such a claim and should not be so maligned. He was a just a man who found himself in an impossible situation and tried to end suffering.\'94 Kleo saw the faces in the silent crowd, wide with expectation and yearning to hear more.\
\'93The nation of Ulyssia occupied this entire continent, 7.6 million square kilometres of golden beaches, pristine bushland packed with mighty hardwoods, clear lakes, wetlands and unique desert landscapes. The unmatched beauty of this land inspired great poets, artisans, playwright and composers, but greed put an end to all of this, turning paradise it into a wasteland, a giant open cut mine, devoid of inspiration and humanity. The oceans were transformed into giant septic tanks oozing filth and belching evil gases from within. Inland waterways were no more than grandiose sewers and marine life only existed as commodities in aqua culture or as single celled animals that somehow managed to survive in the slime. Grand buildings of architectural spendour were replaced by shopping malls and glass towers, statues of great men were re-sculpted into likenesses of uber-rich entrepreneurs and heroes of the stock market. Public spaces evaporated into housing and office blocks towering up to the sky, elevating the rich above and beyond our putrid lives here on the ground.\'94\
\'93What\'92s sky?\'94 The crowd was transfixed by Kleo, but let out a collective chuckle. No one present had ever seen this mythical sky-thing and many were skeptics. \
\'93Something that will never be seen again, Elvis. Ulyssians of old considered themselves somehow removed from the concerns of the rest of the world, but the annihilation of the west coast, a demonstration of childish petulance by the President of the Union of Atlantic Capitalist States, caused a mass migration to the east. Many argued that the mass migration of millions of refugees from the west was the genesis of the new class system, but it was not. It was merely the catalyst, heating up the social chemistry and speeding the rate of collapse of the old order. The landed gentry began to think of themselves as modern day Lords, owning land and anyone on it, without the tiresome burden of having to raise a peasant army to fight in a monarch\'92s war.\'94\
\'93Could not the Dawkins have stopped this?\'94 An Elvis in the front row asked. \
\'93No, not even the Dawkins could stop this. The repressive regime that arose from the ashes of Ulyssia was not interested in imaginings as human dignity, democratisation or raising living standards, it was deliberately and violently opposed to such folly. Corporate fundamentalism insisted a world view in terms of profits only. Concerns for you, the workers, fell away, replaced by exponentially increasing employer influence. Collective bargaining and union involvement was not only forbidden, it was criminalized to the point that most unionists found themselves standing blindfolded, before a pock-marked wall. Employers occupied a powerful position at the negotiating table and few individual employees were adept at bargaining. Government revealed a clear class preference for employers, allowing them to use commercial law to determined conditions in the workplace, lawfully treating workers as commodities with no inherent rights or privileges.\'94\
\'93That sounds like a poor interpretation of constitutional powers, Great Sage.\'94 Another well read Elvis stood to his feet. \
\'93In reaction to the the millions of westerners who would work for half the money, employers gained judicial approval to implement semi-paid slavery. Employers had the ascendancy and were not going to let go of their strangle hold on the industrial landscape. Why would they? It did not go unnoticed that, as workers rights eroded, productivity and profits soared. A happy worker might be an efficient worker, but a frightened worker is a fast worker.\'94\
 \
\'93Nothing has changed!\'94 Another Elvis chuckled from the crowd. \'93We still only get minimum wage.\'94\
\'93But it can change. It must change. In the infancy of the new Primary Trade Dominion, the overwhelming and inescapable characteristic of the corporate life was the inability for any individual, group or even government to engage the management class in any meaningful discourse. To engage the managers, one was obliged to use their language and play by the rules set by management theologians. By using their language, management-speak, you were playing their game and you could not win. There were degrees of losing, with some outcomes more desirable, but there could be no victory. Unionism was dead and no one spoke for the worker.\'94\
\'93Speak for us, Great Sage!\'94 The crowd clapped loudly.\
\'93That is why I am here, members of the Elvi. Managers, bureaucrats, technocrats and power brokers never give you a second thought. If you did not create wealth you were merchandise to the abused until broken. They are all educated in Management-Theology and are all limited by the same world-view. They are not trained to untangle intricacies and nuances of business, rather they are stripped away of all independent thought and tutored only to produce answers that satisfy management theory. There is nothing pragmatic about the methodology. If it works but does not conform to management theory, it has to be changed. Management Theology Colleges are convinced that boardrooms are stuffed with confused, feeble-minded fools who have spent their working lives wandering around aimlessly, waiting for MTC graduates to join the team and solve problems intractable.\'94 Kleo paused and saw the crowd was still enthralled by his take on the history of the Primary Trade Dominion. \'93Although the government catered almost exclusively to the requirements of the market, corporate culture never returned the favour, bleeding away the power of the state. Unfettered profiteering ensured that environmental affairs were permanently on the fringe of political thought, assuming, of course, that concerns for the environment ever entered their heads. When they had realised it was too late, or rather, finally admitted it was unlikely the environment was going to self-correct, executives firmly set their sights on making as much profit as possible before Armageddon. He who had the most toys would win. An industrialist\'92s utopia sprang up in the Primary Trade Dominion where business ventures met with no regulation, no morality and no impediments. Virtually non existent copyright laws left a gaping void that was exploited for all it was worth and duplication of foreign business strategies naturally extended to outright thievery of intellectual property. Our entire society has been based on theft and lies.\'94\
\'93The one-after-the-one will change everything!\'94 An enthusiastic Dawk called out.\
\'93Praise The Dawkins!\'94 The entire Elvi crowd cried out in union, their melodious voices washing warmly over Kleo.\
\'93Yes, my Elvi. Praise the Dawkins.\'94 Kleo raised both arms above his head causing wolf whistles and loud screaming. \'93The one-after-the-one will bring forth the age of Reason. Praise be to him.\'94 He walked off to an ovation of jumping and screaming Elvi. \

